        "type": "thinking",
        "thinking": "I'm planning a treasure hoard prop group in League of Legends style—exaggerated dynamic shapes, saturated glowing accents, hand-painted look readable from a top-down camera. Thinking through the joint structure: most parts stay as plain static objects, with joints reserved only for moving pieces like lids, plus a single root joint to anchor everything.\n\nI'll add a hinge joint for the chest lid and maybe a floating gem for magical glow. For composition, I'm sketching a roughly 2m-wide pile: a gold coin mound with scattered coins on top, an open chest glowing with gold in back, a tilted crown up front, a few goblets (one spilled), and scattered gems in cyan, magenta, emerald, and ruby with glow effects, plus a big hextech-style crystal. I'm also considering extras like a pearl necklace, a half-buried jeweled dagger, and a skull to round out the scene while keeping scope manageable.\n\nNow I'm extending the hoard with goblet and coin stacks, a coin sack, a scepter, and a glowing rune circle decal on the ground beneath everything for a readable top-down silhouette. I'm planning a low stone dais at y=0 to anchor the pile, sizing the chest around 0.9m wide with the lid opening up to 1.2m tall, and laying out coordinates so the chest sits at the back with the coin pile spilling forward. I'm setting up a merge system that groups geometries by color and combines them into a single mesh rooted at origin, making sure all the primitive shapes share consistent position, normal, and UV attributes for merging.\n\nI realize the polyhedron shapes are non-indexed while most primitives are indexed, so I need to convert everything to non-indexed before merging. I'm designing the coin geometry as small flattened cylinders, building a heap using a lathe profile painted with a scales pattern to mimic stacked gold, then scattering individual tilted coins across the surface, while thinking through how textured coin faces with an emblem can coexist with the merged, flat-shaded geometry.\n\nI should merge the circle decal with the coin texture so it stays a single part rather than doubling the count, keeping triangle counts manageable—roughly 10 segments per circle, times however many coins at 400, factoring in the cylinder geometry too.\n\nDropping to 8-sided cylinders to cut triangle count further, I'm weighing whether to apply face decals to only some coins and thinking through how the UV mapping should work: caps get a circular texture mapped within the unit square, while the sides stretch the full image vertically across the coin's thin height.\n\nSince the texture ends up averaged into a thin colored strip around the rim, I can get away with a single textured coin part rather than multiple variants. For differently-colored coins (gold, silver, copper), I'll draw the texture in greyscale so each merged part's tint color determines the final appearance.\n\nFor placing the coins realistically on the heap, I'm going to build my own heightfield rather than relying on surface helpers — defining the pile's shape analytically as overlapping mounds, then scattering coins across it using gradient-based normals for tilt, and constructing the heap itself as a custom displaced mesh from that heightfield.\n\nNow I'm weighing whether this geometry needs UVs for painting to work — since the system likely bakes textures by rasterizing triangles with generated UV layouts, I'm considering starting from a PlaneGeometry (which already has UVs) and displacing its vertices instead of building fully custom geometry, to stay safer.\n\nI'm figuring out the heap's height field: a 28x28 grid across 2.2m gives manageable triangle counts, and I'm defining the shape as a sum of gaussian mounds, with the main pile centered off-origin and rising toward the chest.\n\nI want the mounds to taper smoothly to zero near the plane's edge rather than clipping, and I'm placing a circular stone dais beneath the heap with a rune ring, floor at y=0. For the chest itself I'm setting a box footprint near the heap's front, giving it wooden plank texturing and metal fittings.\n\nI'm figuring out the lid's barrel-vault shape and how its hinge joint at the back should rotate so the front edge lifts up correctly when opened.\n\nThen I'll build the interior: a mound of gold coins inside the chest with an emissive glow suggesting magical treasure light spilling out.\n\nSettling on warm brown wood with gold bands and a cyan hextech glow for the lock gem, and mapping out a full jewel-tone palette: gold, wood brown, dark iron, crimson velvet, and a spread of gem colors (ruby, sapphire, emerald, amethyst, cyan glow, topaz, rose) to carry across the whole scene.\n\nFor the dais stone I'm going dark blue-grey with cyan/gold rune glow so the saturated gold accents pop against the darker base.\n\nNow I'm designing the crown as an oversized, exaggerated piece: a lathed frustum band topped with seven alternating tall and short spikes, each extruded with a fleur-like outline and capped with a gem, built to radiate dramatically around the band.\n\nI need a local coordinate frame for the crown since it may rest tilted on the gold pile, so I'm writing a helper that maps local points and directions into world space via a transform matrix for both the lathe band and the extruded spikes — though it may be simpler to just keep the crown upright sitting on top of the heap instead.\n\nFor details, I'm planning a visible velvet inner dome through the open top, pearls at the spike tips, a big front ruby gem, and a painted diamond-lozenge pattern on the gold band to simulate engraving.\n\nWriting gold paint shading function...\n\nNow I'm designing the goblets as lathed profiles with foot, stem, and bowl — a tall gold hero goblet, a short ruby-studded one, a silver chalice knocked over with spilling coins, and a small accent piece, using a tilt option on the lathe axis for the toppled one.\n\nFor the loose gems I'm mixing octahedrons, small icosahedrons, and a proper brilliant-cut lathe profile with a crown and pavilion, merging geometry by color so the glow effect applies per gem group rather than per individual part.\n\nBut I'm realizing full emissive glow would wash out facet shading entirely, so I need to dial emissiveIntensity down to something like 0.3 to keep facets visible while still reading as brighter than normal gem material.\n\n",
